WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.448
AI agent incidents can include data
leakage, unauthorized tool action,

2
00:00:05.528 --> 00:00:10.476
policy bypass, unsafe output, retrieval
poisoning, excessive access,

3
00:00:10.556 --> 00:00:14.287
broken approval flow, or unexpected memory
behavior.

4
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:20.127
Response teams need evidence that is
reliable enough to reconstruct what happened.

5
00:00:20.207 --> 00:00:25.641
That means relevant session logs,
tool-call records, authorization records,

6
00:00:25.721 --> 00:00:30.588
data access logs, model and prompt version
history, approval records,

7
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:32.291
and timeline evidence.

8
00:00:32.371 --> 00:00:36.589
Evidence preservation should be planned
before an incident.

9
00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:41.130
Teams should know how to freeze relevant
logs, retain trace data,

10
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.347
preserve tool-call outputs, revoke tokens,
disable tools,

11
00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:49.807
suspend memory features, and capture
configuration versions.

12
00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:55.241
They should also know who can access
sensitive incident data and how privacy or

13
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.105
legal teams are involved.

14
00:00:57.185 --> 00:01:01.728
Agent investigations often touch user
content and internal data,

15
00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:04.728
so response must be careful as well as
fast.

16
00:01:04.808 --> 00:01:09.675
Timeline reconstruction is one of the most
valuable uses of agent logs.

17
00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:14.541
Responders need to connect the original
request, retrieved context,

18
00:01:14.621 --> 00:01:19.650
memory reads or writes, model output, tool
selection, policy decisions,

19
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.380
approvals, downstream actions, and final
response.

20
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.921
If the timeline has gaps, root cause
analysis becomes guesswork.

21
00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:32.544
Good logging lets teams distinguish model
error, policy failure,

22
00:01:32.624 --> 00:01:38.059
user approval confusion, tool bug, data
issue, or downstream system behavior.

23
00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:42.113
Governance reporting turns incidents into
improvement.

24
00:01:42.193 --> 00:01:47.628
Lessons learned should update policies,
prompts, detections, tool controls,

25
00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:51.034
approval flows, retention rules, and
training.

26
00:01:51.114 --> 00:01:54.521
Monitoring should feed continuous
improvement,

27
00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:57.440
not merely produce compliance evidence.

28
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.224
The strongest programs use agent logs to
make future behavior safer,

29
00:02:02.304 --> 00:02:04.900
more reliable, and easier to explain.
